WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:09.300
Speaker 1: So I present to you with peace and love&nbsp;
and a word of warning. The latest FiveThirtyEight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.760
estimates for the Senate and for the House for&nbsp;
these midterm elections. Now, there are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.680
different caveats and important things to talk&nbsp;
about. But let me give you the numbers first in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:28.680
the Senate. The FiveThirtyEight forecast now&nbsp;
puts it at a 68% chance that Democrats hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.760
control of the Senate. That is greater than a&nbsp;
2 to 1 margin. Now, things that are 32% likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:42.540
to happen happen all the time. So so we can't&nbsp;
count out Republicans in the Senate. It is 68&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:48.840
to 32 in terms of likelihood of controlling the&nbsp;
Senate. Based on the FiveThirtyEight forecast over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:55.800
in the House. It is a 24% chance that Democrats&nbsp;
hold the House of Representatives. It's roughly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.700
we could say, a 3 to 1 margin by which Republicans&nbsp;
are expected to hold the House. Now, I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.940
remind you guys, this doesn't mean Republicans&nbsp;
are expected to hold 76% of the seats. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:14.700
not at all. It's expected to be a relatively small&nbsp;
margin if Republicans win. But Republicans are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:22.440
favored to win the House by any margin by roughly&nbsp;
3 to 1. Now, there are many important things to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:29.820
talk about here. First and foremost, both of&nbsp;
these numbers, the Democratic odds in the Senate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.260
and the Democratic odds in the House have been&nbsp;
climbing over time. You might remember that some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.720
months ago on the Senate side, it was like a&nbsp;
52% chance that Democrats were going to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:45.840
the Senate. That's basically a coin flip that has&nbsp;
steadily climbed and climbed and climbed. It got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.780
to 2 to 1 a few days ago and it has now gone&nbsp;
beyond 2 to 1. So the momentum is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.000
there in terms of Democrats now. They're not&nbsp;
going to get 60 seats. Democrats are not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.080
to get 60 seats. So in some sense, it's kind&nbsp;
of an academic question whether Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.320
if they do hold the Senate, will have somewhere&nbsp;
between a 5050 majority with Kamala Harris as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.920
the 51st tie breaking vote, up to some number&nbsp;
under 60 senators. 60 is important because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.320
there are some measures in the Senate where you&nbsp;
need 60 votes. A simple majority isn't enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:30.180
Democrats are not going to get 65. I don't see&nbsp;
any path for that. So it is almost certainly going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.260
to be a situation where in order to get things&nbsp;
done, Democrats on many issues are going to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.220
some Republican votes. Obviously, it's better&nbsp;
to only need four Republican votes than ten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.420
No doubt about that. But that's where we're&nbsp;
kind of operating, a very narrow margin now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:51.120
over in the House. When you just look at the House&nbsp;
numbers and you see, oh, man, that three, two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.000
one margin Republicans are expected to hold&nbsp;
just a couple of months ago, the Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:04.020
Democratic odds were far lower. I think they were&nbsp;
around 16, 17 or 18%. So the shift from 17 to 24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.240
in terms of the sheer raw numbers is&nbsp;
not significant. But seven over 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:16.680
you're talking about a roughly 40% increase in the&nbsp;
odds that Democrats hold still favored to lose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:22.920
But a significant change. What isn't reflected&nbsp;
in these numbers? Democratic overperformance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:30.000
and Democratic electorate overrepresentation. In&nbsp;
other words, turnout. And what we have seen now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:41.820
in Michigan and in New York and in Alaska is that&nbsp;
the polls did not reflect a correct weighting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:47.160
the basis of Democratic enthusiasm. And we've&nbsp;
talked before about a number of the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:53.220
reasons for this enthusiasm. Part of it includes,&nbsp;
yes, the overturning of Roe v Wade by this right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:59.040
wing Supreme Court. Part of it includes Republican&nbsp;
inaction on gun safety, although over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.700
month, that's probably faded a little bit in&nbsp;
people's minds. And the very dramatic shift in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:12.600
all of a sudden, the MAGA right is in massive&nbsp;
legal trouble. Trump might be getting indicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:16.800
Republicans, civil war, all of these different&nbsp;
things. And on the on the Democratic side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:23.280
things are quite calm. Joe Biden is racking up&nbsp;
legislative accomplishments. The Pact Act. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.920
Inflation Reduction Act. Infrastructure. A very&nbsp;
good jobs report. We are essentially at full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:35.580
employment. Wages are up. Inflation is down. Gas&nbsp;
prices are down. And all of a sudden, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:42.900
people who are saying, what is the choice here?&nbsp;
A party in disarray, soon facing indictments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.820
The Republican Party or Joe Biden, who has very&nbsp;
quietly gotten a bunch of things done, student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.860
loan debt forgiveness now coming, people talking&nbsp;
about maybe something on cannabis, etc., etc.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:59.040
that is not reflected in the numbers. And so while&nbsp;
historically this should be a very bad election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.840
for Democrats, it's increasingly looking less&nbsp;
and less bad. We're going to continue following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.200
the numbers and I hope you'll join me tonight,&nbsp;
roughly 7 p.m. Eastern for Joe Biden's address to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.000
the nation. I think it will be very interesting. I&nbsp;
will be live on YouTube, Twitch and Facebook. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.340
of course, you can follow me on Twitter at David&nbsp;
Pakman. Let me know what you make of these numbers

